From: Simon Polack <spolack+git@mailbox.org>
Subject: [PATCH] octeon_pip: transmit fraglist GSO packets as they are (NETIF_F_GSO_FRAGLIST)

A forwarded flow arrives as a GRO list: the head carries what the stack changed (the output MAC header, ttl, tos, a NAT's addresses and ports) and the other packets as received, each with its own IP and UDP/TCP header in its own buffer. Without NETIF_F_GSO_FRAGLIST the stack re-segments the list in validate_xmit_skb(): skb_segment_list() gives every segment a copy of the head's skb state (a conntrack reference, the header offsets) and of the MAC header, the L4 walk fixes addresses, ports, ttl, tos and the checksums, inet_gso_segment() ids, lengths and IP checksums, dev_hard_start_xmit() hands each segment to opip_xmit(), the completion frees each after dropping its state: five passes over the segments' lines, 22 % of the forwarding core on the EdgeRouter 4 with a 64 byte UDP flood (630 cycles per packet on image P2), plus 8 % for the per-segment conntrack reference in the NAT case.

Advertise NETIF_F_GSO_FRAGLIST (with NETIF_F_GSO_UDP_L4, NETIF_F_TSO and NETIF_F_TSO_ECN: net_gso_ok() wants a feature for every gso_type bit of a list; opip_features_check() takes all of them away from every GSO packet that is not an admitted list, so a local socket's TSO or UDP GSO is segmented by the stack as before) and send the list in one pass: per segment the head's MAC header (from a local copy) into the room before its IP header, the IP and L4 fix-up the stack's walks do (identical values: the head's addresses, ports, ttl and tos, the head's id plus the segment's index, the segment's own length, the IP checksum recomputed), a PKO command for the buffer from the MAC header to the end, with the PKO's L4 checksum insertion (IPOFFP1, the pseudo-header checksum left in the field) when the addresses or ports changed and the segment has a checksum, unchanged headers keep their verified ones; the head's own packet first with its UDP length, IP length and checksum fixed to its own size. The ring slot of the last command holds the head (OPIP_TX_LIST): the PKO sends in order, so its completion means all segments are out; it frees the head and the segments one by one to the NAPI cache and the page pool's direct path, as single packets are freed, and reports the list's bytes to BQL (kept in the head's cb). The segments never get a copy of the head's state: no conntrack reference, no dst, no header copy per segment.

What takes the old path: everything opip_tx_list_ok() does not admit (IPv6, encapsulated, fixed-id TCP, DODGY, a MAC header longer than two VLAN tags, a CN3XXX half duplex port with runt padding) is segmented by the stack as before; a list with a segment that has page fragments or no room for the MAC header, or longer than the queue's wake threshold, is segmented in the driver with skb_gso_segment() and sent segment by segment; a list that does not fit the queue's free slots stops the queue and returns NETDEV_TX_BUSY (the wake threshold is the longest list, so a woken queue has room). The XDP, netpoll and pktgen paths are untouched; a purge frees a list's head with its list. GRO only lists IPv4 headers without options that are no fragments with a verified checksum (inet_gro_receive), so ihl is 5 for every segment, and the segments' header offsets are GRO's, which the stack's skb_segment_list() relies on the same way.

Correctness is end to end on the bench: the NAT UDP flood (checksums at the host), the stack flood (ids, lengths), the TCP tests (the TCP fraglist path), the VLAN case (an 18 byte MAC header), WireGuard/GRE (the lists arrive at the tunnel device: software path, unchanged).

v4: the list head's entry no longer goes through the gather mapping of opip_tx_write() (v3 rewrote the last segment's command into a gather of the whole list, or left it unwritten when the WQE pool allocation failed, and stored the list pointer over the BQL bytes in the head's cb); a list that does not fit the queue announces the batch's earlier commands (doorbell, poll list or timer) before it stops the queue and returns NETDEV_TX_BUSY (v3 stopped the queue with those commands unannounced: nothing rang them later, the queue never woke: "N packets in flight, the PKO holds 0 command words"); a head whose own packet is not exactly one segment (headlen - MAC - headers != gso_size, or page fragments) goes through the stack's segmentation.

v5 (review): a cloned head or a shared segment goes through the stack's segmentation (the headers are written in place and the segments freed as the list's own; a tap on the ingress port, ipmr or a bridge flood clone the head and copy-on-write shares the segments); the gather list of a fragmented skb is bounded to its buffer (WARN_ONCE, dropped); ethtool -S tx_list_geometry and tx_list_shared count the lists handed to the stack from the list path.

Signed-off-by: Simon Polack <spolack+git@mailbox.org>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/cavium/octeon/octeon_pip.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/cavium/octeon/octeon_pip.h
@@ -203,6 +203,13 @@ struct opip_txq {
 	unsigned long no_wqe;
 	unsigned long no_chunk;
 	unsigned long stopped;
+	/*
+	 * Fraglist GSO packets handed to the stack's segmentation from
+	 * opip_xmit_list(): a head whose own packet is not one segment, a
+	 * cloned head or shared segments, see there
+	 */
+	unsigned long list_geometry;
+	unsigned long list_shared;
 	unsigned long ring[OPIP_TXQ_RING];
 	/* Page pool skbs netpoll completed with irqs off, for the NAPI */
 	struct sk_buff *netpoll_skbs;
